{
  "requirement": "`trust_but_verify::outline::Span` locates text by byte offsets from the start\nof the original source snapshot, including when a caller selects a nested item.\n`start` is inclusive and `end` is exclusive. A valid span satisfies\n`start <= end <= source.len()` and both offsets are UTF-8 character boundaries.\nFor `source: &str`, `&source[span]` yields the same slice as\n`&source[span.start..span.end]`, with the same bounds and boundary checks.\n\n```rust,ignore\n#[non_exhaustive]\npub struct Span {\n    pub start: usize,\n    pub end: usize,\n}\n\nimpl Span {\n    pub fn new(start: usize, end: usize) -> Self;\n}\n```\n\nTrait implementations\n\n```rust,ignore\nimpl Clone for Span;\nimpl Copy for Span;\nimpl Eq for Span;\nimpl PartialEq for Span;\nimpl Debug for Span;\nimpl Index<Span> for str {\n    type Output = str;\n}\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The reviewed tests cover each obligation in `requirements.sdk.outlines.Span`.\n\n**Indexing and boundary checks.** `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/outline_content.rs#fn[span_indexes_str_at_utf8_byte_boundaries]` uses the source \"aé🦀\".\n- For the valid spans (0,0), (1,3) and (3,7), it asserts `&source[span] == &source[span.start..span.end]`.\n- It asserts `&source[Span::new(1,3)] == \"é\"`, which shows start is inclusive and end exclusive.\n- It asserts `&source[Span::new(7,7)] == \"\"` for an empty span at the end.\n- It checks that indexing panics for five invalid spans. (2,3) has its start inside a character. (0,2) has its end inside a character. (2,2) has both offsets inside a character. (0,8) has an end past `len`. (3,1) has `start > end`. Together these cover every part of the validity rule `start <= end <= len` plus both boundary conditions.\n\n**Trait implementations.**\n- `Eq` is checked by `needs_eq::<Span>()`.\n- `Index<Span> for str` with `Output = str` is exercised by the indexing assertions.\n- `Span::new` is checked by the `span.start` and `span.end` reads and by the panic on `Span::new(3,1)`, which shows it does not normalise its arguments.\n- `PartialEq` and `Debug` are exercised by `assert_eq!` on `Span` values in `unicode_spans_use_bytes_and_whitespace_only_lines_create_no_items`.\n- `Copy` and `Clone` are exercised because spans are reused after being indexed and iterated by value.\n\n**`#[non_exhaustive]` and public fields.** The `compile_fail,E0639` doctest on `crates/trust-but-verify/src/outline/contents.rs#struct[Span]` checks that a caller cannot build `Span` with a struct literal. The public `start` and `end` fields are read directly in the tests. `public_api.rs#fn[public_exports_match_the_appendix]` also checks the export inventory.\n\n**Byte offsets from the original snapshot, including nested items.**\n- `unicode_spans_use_bytes_and_whitespace_only_lines_create_no_items` asserts byte offsets (0,2) and (6,10) for multibyte lines, and `&source[item.span]` equals the selected text.\n- `setext_sections_inside_directives_lists_and_quotes_retain_container_spans` asserts that nested items inside directives, lists and quotes have absolute `span.start` and `name_span` values. It also asserts that the selected text equals `&source[item.span]`.\n\nI found no unmarked-test or assertion gaps.",
  "references": "9476ad0b4b7916d5a599cabbd1fdfd423dde845652fb244124abadeee83ca75a",
